WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:01:22.369 --> 00:01:27.630
Let me ask you a question: Which do you prefer:
the country or the city?

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:36.639
Ok, I’m sure your answer will depend on
what activity you have in mind.

00:01:36.639 --> 00:01:40.740
If we want to spend a few days away from the
hustle and bustle of the city, we’d probably

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:44.170
choose the countryside,

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:52.299
but if instead we think about day-to-day life,
undertaking our own business projects or going

00:01:52.299 --> 00:01:55.890
on a night out, the answer may change completely…

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:59.090
Don’t fool yourself.

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:02.090
We all like cities.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:03.119
And that has consequences.

00:02:03.119 --> 00:02:13.840
For the first time ever in history, more than
half of the world's population lives in cities.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:18.230
Cities have emerged as the nuclei in which
people, completely unknown to each other and

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:24.940
of various origins, coexist and coordinate
their lives to fulfill their goals.

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:31.290
Who hasn’t felt amazed by the hustle and
bustle of London, New York or Tokyo?

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:37.790
A huge mix of cultures, religions and nationalities.

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:42.500
Cities are made up of very different people,
but many have something in common: the desire

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:44.900
to grow as a society.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:48.489
Isn’t that amazing?

00:02:48.489 --> 00:02:52.599
Without thinking about it, or in many cases
without even being aware of it, we contribute

00:02:52.599 --> 00:02:57.590
to the welfare of a lot of people we don’t
even know, and they do the same in return,

00:02:57.590 --> 00:03:03.410
by providing a lot of services that we need.

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:08.350
It is precisely for this reason, folks, that
cities have been consolidated as the axis,

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:14.669
the nucleus, the engine of the freest and
most prosperous human society model that humanity

00:03:14.669 --> 00:03:19.769
has ever known.

00:03:19.769 --> 00:03:23.739
But does the growth of cities today pose a
threat to the rural world?

00:03:23.739 --> 00:03:26.660
Are cities meeting the needs of their residents?

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:30.890
How is global urbanization affecting people’s
lives?

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:35.459
Let's take a look!

00:03:35.459 --> 00:03:41.709
(FROM THE COUNTRYSIDE TO THE CITY)

00:03:41.709 --> 00:03:48.799
I’m sure many of you have grandparents or
great-grandparents who were born and lived

00:03:48.799 --> 00:03:49.799
in a village.

00:03:49.799 --> 00:03:56.590
Because, folks, the migration from the countryside
to the city has been a relatively recent phenomenon.

00:03:56.590 --> 00:04:00.950
You all know this, historically, humans lived
scattered in small communities where life

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:05.090
was based on subsistence farming and whatever
products they were able to produce.

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:09.280
If you were lucky, you might have your own
plot of land, where you’d raise a few animals

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:13.540
and plant vegetables, which you’d later
consume.

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:15.590
As you can imagine, life was very, very hard.

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:19.799
People spent their lives working from childhood
to try to get ahead, often with no chance

00:04:19.799 --> 00:04:21.639
of ever securing a better life.

00:04:21.639 --> 00:04:28.820
And, what’s more, the risk of external factors
making life worse was extremely high.

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:32.830
For example, drought or flood could lead to
a terrible famine that could end the lives

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:35.340
of thousands and thousands of people.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:38.029
Not to mention wars, invasion and disease.

00:04:38.029 --> 00:04:43.099
That’s why, among other things, mortality
was so high in the past.

00:04:43.099 --> 00:04:46.950
So, would you like to go back to the good
old days?

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:47.950
Surely not...

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:53.219
Come on, don’t kid yourself, in 99% of cases,
the answer will be no.

00:04:53.219 --> 00:05:01.830
In general, cities offer us a higher quality
of life and a range of opportunities.

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:06.020
It all began with industrialization, back
in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries,

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:09.500
which also lead to a boom in trade and technological
innovations.

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:18.539
In that era, cities began to grow exponentially,
by attracting millions of people from the

00:05:18.539 --> 00:05:21.470
countryside who wanted to improve their living
conditions.

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:26.930
And that's the definition of urbanization,
the massive transfer of people from the countryside

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:36.400
to the city that has been happening continuously
for more than two centuries.

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:41.349
A process that has allowed many completely
new economic activities to flourish, improving

00:05:41.349 --> 00:05:45.330
everyone's lives.

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:49.960
Since as recent as 1960, the world’s urban
population has multiplied by four, while the

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:56.180
rural population has grown very slowly, and
has almost reached tipping point.

00:05:56.180 --> 00:06:02.889
It’s estimated that between 2025 and 2030
the world's rural population will begin to

00:06:02.889 --> 00:06:06.020
shrink for the first time in the history of
mankind.

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:11.759
So far it has decreased in relative terms,
but now it’ll also begin to do so in absolute

00:06:11.759 --> 00:06:16.590
terms.

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:22.759
Basically, we live in a world of cities.

00:06:22.759 --> 00:06:25.030
Now, how may this change affect us?

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:30.479
Obviously, in order to live, we need goods
produced in the countryside, in particular

00:06:30.479 --> 00:06:31.479
food.

00:06:31.479 --> 00:06:36.479
And this leads us to a big question that comes
up every once in a while:

00:06:36.479 --> 00:06:39.990
Can a world of cities feed the planet’s
growing population?

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:43.630
Does this urbanization process threaten our
food security?

00:06:43.630 --> 00:06:44.630
Hmmm...

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:50.759
A lot of people say yes, but we’re not so
sure about it.

00:06:50.759 --> 00:06:58.639
Folks, a question we need to ask ourselves
is, are we producing food in the same way

00:06:58.639 --> 00:06:59.960
as we did 50 years ago?

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:02.389
The short answer is no, we’re not.

00:07:02.389 --> 00:07:07.661
Nowadays we don’t need 10, 20, or 30 farmers
to plough a couple of hectares of land, a

00:07:07.661 --> 00:07:16.240
John Deere 8285R is more than enough.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:17.540
This has a name: mechanization.

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:24.550
The new technologies, which, incidentally,
tend to be developed in the cities, have brought

00:07:24.550 --> 00:07:29.680
tremendous improvements to the rural environment,
improvements that of course translate into

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:31.020
a better quality of life.

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:34.919
In the countryside, mechanization has greatly
reduced the number of workers required in

00:07:34.919 --> 00:07:40.639
the field, not only maintaining but even increasing
agricultural production.

00:07:40.639 --> 00:07:45.770
That’s exactly why Malthusian theories,
the theories that claim that the world is

00:07:45.770 --> 00:07:49.740
suffering from overpopulation and that we
won’t have the resources to feed everyone,

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:52.439
fail over and over and over again.

00:07:52.439 --> 00:07:56.740
However, hold on a second folks, because as
you can imagine, this population explosion

00:07:56.740 --> 00:08:01.229
creates many, many challenges in the cities,
especially in developing countries.

00:08:01.229 --> 00:08:05.539
The main one is urban poverty, caused among
other things by the scarcity of housing that

00:08:05.539 --> 00:08:10.379
occurs in extreme cases of urban explosion,
which we’ll take a closer look at in a moment.

00:08:10.379 --> 00:08:14.730
But despite these challenges and problems,
have you ever wondered, what exactly is the

00:08:14.730 --> 00:08:19.789
big attraction that cities offer that lead
so many people to move to them?

00:08:19.789 --> 00:08:21.659
Well...

00:08:21.659 --> 00:08:25.400
Listen up.

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:29.540
(CITIES, COOPERATION HUBS)

00:08:29.540 --> 00:08:32.289
Why did industrialization emerge in cities?

00:08:32.289 --> 00:08:38.160
Why are the head offices of major companies
always in the largest cities?

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:43.950
It’s not only about having access to better
infrastructure, such as roads, ports or airports,

00:08:43.950 --> 00:08:48.010
but also about having easy access to all of
us, that is, to human resources.

00:08:48.010 --> 00:08:54.400
Folks, companies need a lot of different profiles,
different people, to be able to function.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:59.270
Have you ever wondered how many people are
involved in the creation of just one everyday

00:08:59.270 --> 00:09:00.270
product?

00:09:00.270 --> 00:09:01.270
Say for example, a car.

00:09:01.270 --> 00:09:05.380
You need engineers, mechanics, designers,
even leather tanners and artisans for the

00:09:05.380 --> 00:09:07.790
vehicle’s interior details.

00:09:07.790 --> 00:09:12.980
That’s precisely why the biggest cities,
where more people of different profiles coexist,

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:15.680
are the ideal place to find human resources.

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:20.020
The more professionals and different profiles
there are, the more attractive the place will

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:21.020
be.

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:25.740
Cities have become true hubs of human cooperation,
where we fill the needs of others, and they,

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:29.270
in turn, supply us with what we lack.

00:09:29.270 --> 00:09:34.440
All this without even knowing us, isn’t
that incredible?

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:39.670
These hubs of businesses, entrepreneurship
and consumers have triggered economic growth.

00:09:39.670 --> 00:09:46.589
Now, at this point there’s a question we
need to ask ourselves here on VisualPolitik:

00:09:46.589 --> 00:09:49.400
Where is this phenomenon happening most markedly?

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:50.630
Do you know?

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:52.800
I’m sure it won’t surprise you.

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:53.830
Yes, exactly, in Asia.

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:57.800
But... hold on, because Africa is already
catching up.

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:00.490
Yes, Africa!

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:04.470
And, folks, this is hardly surprising.

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:08.760
Since the beginning of urbanization, the income
per capita for the most urbanized Asian countries

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:10.670
has skyrocketed.

00:10:10.670 --> 00:10:13.200
Urbanization is directly related to economic
development.

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:17.510
The great amount of knowledge, goods and services
exchanges that take place in cities, was something

00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:19.810
unknown to mankind until very recently.

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:26.950
They’re something of a modern miracle.

00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:31.070
Asian countries that have experienced greater
migrations from the countryside to the city

00:10:31.070 --> 00:10:33.970
have seen their citizens’ per capita income
skyrocket.

00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:42.950
That’s the case in South Korea, Thailand,
Japan and Malaysia, among others.

00:10:42.950 --> 00:10:43.990
Check out this chart.

00:10:43.990 --> 00:10:47.960
The greater the concentration of inhabitants,
the higher the country’s per capita income.

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:53.670
And the variation in just 10 years, between
2000 and 2010, leaves no room for doubt.

00:10:53.670 --> 00:10:55.339
But, these aren’t isolated cases.

00:10:55.339 --> 00:11:02.000
This phenomenon has extended throughout the
developed world.

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:07.000
Western Europe, for example, was by far the
first part of the world to experience urbanization,

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:11.050
doubling and even tripling the rates of urbanization
found in the rest of the world.

00:11:11.050 --> 00:11:14.610
In other words, to a large extent, the old
continent’s success can be attributed to

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:18.070
its pioneering bet on city models.

00:11:18.070 --> 00:11:24.000
In fact, with this data in hand, the first
thing that may come to mind is how it is surprising

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:28.810
that it took so long for the other regions
to join this phenomenon, and yet achieve similar

00:11:28.810 --> 00:11:29.810
results so quickly.

00:11:29.810 --> 00:11:31.620
And it's not just an economic issue.

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:32.630
Not at all!

00:11:32.630 --> 00:11:47.190
Science, culture, and political freedoms have
almost always originated in cities.

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:51.540
Cooperation between human beings is easier
and simpler in these spaces, making it easier

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:55.760
for creativity, serendipity and influential
political movements to emerge.

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:57.720
And of course, this is no surprise.

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:00.940
In a village being different or thinking differently
can make you an outcast.

00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:06.060
In cities you become one more, part of an
artistic, intellectual or political movement.

00:12:06.060 --> 00:12:09.199
It's that simple.

00:12:09.199 --> 00:12:11.470
Now, hold on just a second.

00:12:11.470 --> 00:12:16.450
How can we accommodate millions of rural people
in cities without having the necessary housing,

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:18.389
infrastructure and services?

00:12:18.389 --> 00:12:24.790
In India, they know a bit about this challenge.

00:12:24.790 --> 00:12:30.050
(INDIA, EXPLOSIVE URBANIZATION)

00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:34.130
Folks, we’ve already talked about this here
on VisualPolitik.

00:12:34.130 --> 00:12:37.460
India is starting to become a manufacturing
power.

00:12:37.460 --> 00:12:41.440
It’s beginning to take its first steps towards
becoming China’s competitor.

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:44.649
For example, Apple has already announced that
it’ll stop manufacturing the new iPhone

00:12:44.649 --> 00:12:47.080
in China, to do it in India instead.

00:12:47.080 --> 00:12:48.500
Incredible, right?

00:12:48.500 --> 00:12:53.430
Foxconn Is Said Close to Trial Output of Newest
iPhones in India.

00:12:53.430 --> 00:12:55.940
Bloomberg
And of course, India’s industrialization

00:12:55.940 --> 00:13:00.529
will require a lot of workers, people who
are arriving en masse from the countryside

00:13:00.529 --> 00:13:05.339
to the cities.

00:13:05.339 --> 00:13:10.010
The urbanization phenomenon is happening so
rapidly, that in India and in many other cities

00:13:10.010 --> 00:13:15.089
of the developing world, guaranteeing access
to basic services such as electricity, running

00:13:15.089 --> 00:13:19.880
water, education, health and infrastructure
is posing gigantic problems, as it requires

00:13:19.880 --> 00:13:21.390
huge investment.

00:13:21.390 --> 00:13:26.110
And this investment, folks, poses quite a
challenge in some countries.

00:13:26.110 --> 00:13:31.390
For example, Delhi gets around 700,000 new
residents every year, and already has more

00:13:31.390 --> 00:13:35.449
than 27 million inhabitants in its metropolitan
area.

00:13:35.449 --> 00:13:40.470
Its growth is so astronomical that by 2028
it could become the largest megacity in the

00:13:40.470 --> 00:13:43.370
world, surpassing even Tokyo.

00:13:43.370 --> 00:13:45.889
But just a second…

00:13:45.889 --> 00:13:50.130
How can homes for 700,000 people be built
in a single year?

00:13:50.130 --> 00:13:54.350
And what’s more, how can it be done year
after year?

00:13:54.350 --> 00:13:59.269
In India, urbanization is happening very quickly,
and this entails difficulties in guaranteeing

00:13:59.269 --> 00:14:01.680
access to housing for all the people arriving
in the city.

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:06.089
Due to this, it’s estimated that 24% of
the urban population lives in shanty towns

00:14:06.089 --> 00:14:08.570
without even the most basic living conditions.

00:14:08.570 --> 00:14:12.710
Basically, to accommodate all of Delhi’s
new inhabitants, you’d need to build between

00:14:12.710 --> 00:14:18.430
700 and 900 million square meters every year.

00:14:18.430 --> 00:14:25.310
That works out to something roughly the size
of Chicago each year.

00:14:25.310 --> 00:14:31.620
Is this even possible?

00:14:31.620 --> 00:14:34.009
It seems like a complicated challenge…

00:14:34.009 --> 00:14:38.509
And unfortunately, many newcomers are forced
to live in shanty towns, better known as "slums".

00:14:38.509 --> 00:14:43.339
In Lalbagh, the largest slum in Delhi, more
than 300,000 people live in a single square

00:14:43.339 --> 00:14:44.889
kilometer.

00:14:44.889 --> 00:14:47.990
Quite horrific.

00:14:47.990 --> 00:14:51.221
Despite the Indian Government’s efforts
to build houses to relocate the poorest, the

00:14:51.221 --> 00:14:56.050
challenge is so huge that it’s like fighting
against the clock to reduce something that

00:14:56.050 --> 00:15:01.540
doesn’t stop growing.

00:15:01.540 --> 00:15:03.449
How will India deal with this problem?

00:15:03.449 --> 00:15:07.160
We'll talk about this in a later video, here,
on VisualPolitik.

